# Fleet workspace map: local sibling directory name -> owner/repo
#
# WHAT THIS SOLVES
#
# Rust workspaces in this fleet path-depend on crates in OTHER repos, spelled
# as relative paths in Cargo.toml:
#
#   kriyal-base/common  ->  path = "../../../build/..."          (12 manifests)
#   kriyal-base/common  ->  path = "../../../common-device/..."
#   indicnode-resources/artifacts -> crate `base`, in kriyal-base/common
#
# A checkout of one repo therefore cannot build on its own: cargo resolves
# `../../../build` against the filesystem and gets "The system cannot find the
# path specified. (os error 3)". Measured on the first real dispatches to
# kriyalci1 (kriyal-base/common run 33365628968, artifacts run 33365891378) -
# every toolchain step passed and the workspace still could not resolve.
#
# A relative path names a DIRECTORY, not a repository, so nothing in the
# checked-out tree says which repo `../../../build` should have come from.
# That mapping exists only here.
#
# WHY A FLEET-LEVEL FILE RATHER THAN PER-REPO CONFIG
#
# The set of siblings a workspace needs is a property of its own Cargo.toml
# and changes whenever someone adds a path-dep. Hand-maintained lists (a
# per-repo .github/ci-config.yml, or a table in the dashboard) drift silently
# from that the moment code changes, and the build then fails in a repo other
# than the one that changed.
#
# So reusable-build.yml DERIVES the sibling list by scanning the checked-out
# Cargo.toml files, and uses this file only to answer the one question the
# scan cannot: which repo owns a given directory name. That question changes
# when a REPO is added or moved - rarely, and visibly - not when code changes.
#
# FORMAT
#
#   <directory-name> <owner>/<repo>
#
# Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Directory names are the
# last path element a Cargo.toml path-dep resolves to, which by convention in
# this fleet equals the repo name.
#
# Generated 2026-08-31 from the `origin` remote of every clone in the
# development workspace, so it reflects what the repos actually are rather
# than what anyone remembered. Add a line when a new repo joins the fleet.

artifacts             indicnode-resources/artifacts
